.TH DANBY-TWOSLIT 1
.SH NAME
danby-twoslit \- two slits, and the pattern they make
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B danby/twoslit
.SH MODEL
Feynman called the double slit the one phenomenon that contains the only
mystery.  This draws what it produces: fringes, under an envelope, with
the two coming from different things.
.PP
Send particles through two slits of width w a distance d apart and count
where they land.  A single particle arrives at one spot; send enough and
the pattern that builds up is
.PP
.EX
I = [ sin(beta)/beta ]^2  cos^2(alpha)
alpha = pi d sin(theta) / lambda
beta  = pi w sin(theta) / lambda
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Danby/twoslit
is a
.IR danbylab (2)
script \(em the whole model is the text of
.BR /appl/danby/twoslit.spec .
The x axis is position across the screen, not time.
.SS Two effects, not one
The
.B cos squared
is INTERFERENCE.  It comes from the two paths differing by d sin theta,
and it is the part with no classical account: each particle goes through
both slits, in the only sense in which that sentence means anything.
.PP
The
.B sinc squared
is DIFFRACTION.  It comes from the width of a single slit, and it is the
envelope the fringes sit under.  It would be there with one slit.
.PP
Set
.B separation
to zero and the fringes vanish while the envelope stays: that is one
slit, and it is the model's own control.  The test makes it, and also
makes the other half \(em that with both slits the intensity really does
depart from the envelope, or the first check would pass on a model that
had stopped interfering at all.
.SS The missing orders
Worth hunting.  When d is a whole multiple of w, a fringe maximum lands
exactly on an envelope zero and simply is not there: the interference
says put light here, the diffraction says there is none to put.  Set
.B separation
to 2 and
.B slit_width
to 1 and count the gaps.
.SS The angles are exact
sin(theta) is y/sqrt(L^2 + y^2) here and not the paraxial y/L, so the
fringes are exactly where d sin(theta) = n lambda and the envelope
vanishes exactly where w sin(theta) = n lambda.  The familiar spacing
lambda L/d is what those become near the axis; it is visibly wrong at
the edges of a short screen, and shortening
.B distance
shows it going wrong.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/danby/twoslit.spec
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR danby (1),
.IR danby-packet (1),
.IR danby-tunnel (1),
.IR danbylab (2)
.SH BUGS
This is the pattern, not the arrival.  It draws where particles go in
the aggregate and never shows one landing, which is the half that makes
the double slit strange: the pattern is built of individual, localised,
random-looking events and appears even when they are sent one at a time.
Nothing here has a particle in it.
.PP
Both slits are always open and nothing observes them.  The other half of
the mystery \(em that finding out which slit destroys the fringes \(em
needs a detector in the model and there is none.
.PP
Far field, infinitely long slits, one wavelength, no source size.  A
real beam has a spread of wavelengths and a finite source, and both blur
the fringes; the envelope of a real pattern is never quite this clean.
.PP
sin(beta)/beta is 0/0 on the axis, and the answer there is 1.  It is
written as sin(bb)/bb with bb = |beta| + 1e\-12, which is the same
function everywhere else because sine is odd and this is squared.
Guarding only the denominator is the obvious thing and leaves the hole
exactly where it matters, returning zero at the brightest point on the
screen.  It did, until the test asked for the central maximum.
